BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Social
DESCRIPTION:Reed Zine Fest 2026 is the 3rd annual zine event at Reed Colleg
 e (Kaul Auditorium) brought to you by the Reed Zine and Arts Program throug
 h the Reed College Library. RZF invites over 100+ local community artists a
 nd student zinesters\, keynote speakers\, and free art workshops. This year
 's theme is ZINEWRECKED\, think mermaids\, shipwrecks\, aquatic animals\, o
 cean. Come dressed up on theme! \n\n \n\n11am: RZF Opens! (Kaul Auditorium)
 \n\n12pm-4pm: Art Workshops (Performing Arts Building). Sign up for spots a
 t the Welcome Table in Kaul Auditorium. \n\nDrum Leaf Binding Workshop with
  Sarah Joy Calpo\, 12-1pmComics 2.0 Tips and Tricks Workshop with Marc West
 on\, 1-2pmLet's Day Dream Together: Centering Radical Imagination and Conne
 ction with Envy Estacio\, 3-4pm \n\n12pm-2pm: Mini Keynotes (Performing Art
 s Building). Sign up for spots at the Welcome Table in Kaul Auditorium. \n\
 nZines in the classroom? How to incorporate DIY zines into learning assessm
 ent\, with Professor Megan Brunn (Psychology)Disrupting Institutional Narra
 tives with Archival Research\, with Beq Yonaka \n\n2:30-4:00pm: Keynote Tal
 k with special guest Bre of BrattyxBre (PAB 320)\, a Black and Polynesian a
 rtist based in Long Beach\, California\, and has been creating zines and te
 aching others about zines since 2016. Bre reguarily uploads free zine resou
 rces and tutorials onto her YouTube Channel\, creates exclusive monthly zin
 es for her Zine Club on Patreon\, and spends most weekends selling her zine
 s at in-person events nearby. \n\n4pm: RZF Wraps! \n\n \n\nMore information
  can be found at the Reed Zine Library site or on our Instagram @reedzineli
 brary. This is a masked event (please bring your own KN-95/N-95\, limited a
 mount will be available at the Welcome Table)\, though please note that the
  campus is open to the public and therefore you may encounter non-masked pe
 ople on campus. \n\nThis is a free event\, all ages\, open to the public! P
 lease share this with your friends.
DTEND:20260328T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260328T180000Z
GEO:45.481263;-122.633328
LOCATION:Kaul Auditorium
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Reed Zine Fest 2026
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_50694492690068
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/reed-zine-fest-2026
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Lecture
DESCRIPTION:As a Visiting Assistant Professor at Reed College\, I have used
  zines as a science communication assignment across multiple semesters and 
 courses. Incorporating zines into your curriculum has multiple benefits for
  the students. For example\, having students make zines teaches them a new 
 skill and gives an opportunity to bring creativity to their academic work. 
 In this talk\, I will discuss many of the benefits of a zine assignment\, a
 s well as some of the obstacles. I will also explain how I use zines in my 
 curriculum and how I address those obstacles\, such as sharing my approach 
 to a zine-grading rubric. This talk will also explore some of the common qu
 estions and concerns I receive from other educators about using zines as a 
 science communication assessment.\n\nMegan Bruun (she/her) is a Visiting As
 sistant Professor in the Psychology Department at Reed College.\n\nThis Ree
 d Zine Fest Mini Keynote is led by Reed Visiting Assistant Professor Megan 
 Brunn and is being held in PAB 332 (Performing Arts Building\, Room 332).
DTEND:20260328T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260328T190000Z
GEO:45.480983;-122.634105
LOCATION:Performing Arts Building\, PAB 332
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:RZF Mini Keynote: Zines in the classroom? How to incorporate DIY zi
 nes into learning assessment
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52375585437077
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/rzf-mini-keynote-zines-in-the-classroom-h
 ow-to-incorporate-diy-zines-into-learning-assessment
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Workshop/Training
DESCRIPTION:One lazy zinester's favorite way to bind! Let's make drum leaf-
 bound zines!\n\nDrum leaf binding is an adhesive-only method for binding pa
 ges together that lies flat\, lets you avoid dealing with rearranging sprea
 ds for printing\, and is just a really fun way to bind. It's great for bind
 ing things like photographs or art into a cute and chunky book.\n\nThis Ree
 d Zine Fest workshop is led by Sarah Joy Calpo and is being held in PAB 104
  (Performing Arts Building\, Room 104).
DTEND:20260328T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260328T190000Z
GEO:45.480983;-122.634105
LOCATION:Performing Arts Building\, 104
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:RZF: Drum Leaf Binding Workshop
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52298005723657
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/reed-zine-fest-drum-leaf-binding-workshop
 -pab-104
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Lecture
DESCRIPTION:Gather\, Hold\, Ground is an on going traveling project where a
 n art installation is used to display a zine archive. GHG\, is the gatherin
 g of people\, where we can hold space together for the purpose of grounding
  ourselves in each other’s stories through zines. \n\nOriginally from Torre
 on Coahuila\, Mexico\, Lilia Hernandez Galusha\, is a mother\, interdiscipl
 inary artist and storyteller. She grew up in Arkansas\, but has lived in Po
 rtland\, OR\, since 2017. Lilia graduated with a BFA from the University of
  Arkansas in 2012 and later obtained her MFA from the Applied Craft + Desig
 n program in Portland\, OR. Her interdisciplinary work consists of printmak
 ing\, textiles\, drawing\, zine and bookbinding and whatever medium is need
 ed to tell a story. She tells stories of immigration\, family\, motherhood 
 and self-discovery. She believes it's important to exchange our stories so 
 we can better understand the stories our bodies never experienced and conne
 ct to the people with similar narratives. Lilia’s art practice is a continu
 ous effort in trying to link the hand\, mind and heart for the purpose of h
 ealing herself while connecting with others.\n\nThis Reed Zine Fest Mini Ke
 ynote is hosted by Lilia Hernandez Galusha and is being held in PAB 332 (Pe
 rforming Arts Building\, Room 332).
DTEND:20260328T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260328T193000Z
GEO:45.480983;-122.634105
LOCATION:Performing Arts Building\, PAB 332
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:RZF Mini Keynote: Gather\, Hold\, Ground: Building community throug
 h zine installations
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52375471584152
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/rzf-mini-keynote-gather-hold-ground-build
 ing-community-through-zine-installations
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Lecture
DESCRIPTION:Learn techniques for researching underrepresented histories thr
 ough archives and records. Beq will demonstrate how they created a zine on 
 working class student activists and organizing using student group archives
 \, Reed collections\, and community journalism.\n\nBeq (they/them/ze/hir) i
 s a jeweler\, leatherworker\, and creative nonfiction zinester. They are a 
 member of the Iridescent Daydream artist collective\, a LiUNA 483 union ste
 ward\, and a '23 Reed Alum. Hir areas of interest/practice include leather 
 culture\, disability justice\, labor organizing\, and community archives. Z
 e can be found online @wheelchairworm and at Portland area pop-up craft mar
 kets.\n\nThis Reed Zine Fest Mini Keynote is hosted by Reed Alumn Beq Yonak
 a and is being held in PAB 332 (Performing Arts Building\, Room 332).
DTEND:20260328T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260328T200000Z
