BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Lecture
DESCRIPTION:Early Access to Social Security Wealth: A Welfare Analysis -\nT
 his work studies a modification to Social Security that distributes a unive
 rsal lump-\nsum transfer to individuals early in life in exchange for an ac
 tuarially fair reduction\nin retirement benefits. The transfer is welfare-e
 nhancing\, particularly for credit-\nconstrained individuals. Using survey 
 data\, we first document persistent disparities\nin credit access by income
  and race. Next we evaluate the transfer policy in a\nquantitative overlapp
 ing-generations life-cycle model with incomplete markets and\nrace and educ
 ation heterogeneity. We find that a lump-sum transfer of $40\,000\nat age 2
 5 generates positive welfare gains for all race-education groups\, equivale
 nt\nto a 1.0–2.6 percent increase in lifetime consumption. Welfare gains ar
 e driven by\nimproved consumption smoothing and reduced borrowing costs\, a
 nd they decline\nsharply if the transfer is introduced later in life. We sh
 ow that interactions with\nmeans-tested programs\, differential mortality\,
  and bequest motives matter for the\nmagnitude\, but not the sign\, of welf
 are effects. Overall\, a universal program that\ntransfers a portion of an 
 individual’s Social Security wealth earlier in life improves\nwelfare witho
 ut increasing government outlays.
DTEND:20260407T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260407T224000Z
GEO:45.480972;-122.630792
LOCATION:Eliot Hall\, 314
SEQUENCE:0
