BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Lecture
DESCRIPTION:Join the Biology department as seniors share their thesis resea
 rch and discuss their their experiences thoughout their thesis journey.\n\n
 Nanati Fetene Safawo ‘26 -  Neuroscience\, Applewhite Lab “From Surface to 
 Signal: Substrate-Dependent Formation of the Axon Initial Segment in Drosop
 hila Neurons”\n\nThe axon initial segment (AIS) is a critical structure tha
 t maintains neuronal polarity and initiates action potentials. Proper AIS f
 ormation is essential for normal neuronal function\, and disruptions to its
  organization have been implicated in various neuropathologies. Our study i
 nvestigates how extracellular cues influence AIS formation in Drosophila ne
 urons. Our findings suggest that substrate composition directly influences 
 both neuronal morphology and AIS organization\, highlighting the importance
  of extracellular signaling in shaping neuronal architecture and function.\
 n\n​​Meredith Hastings ‘26  --  ES-Biology\, Fey Lab “Impacts of the 2020 L
 abor Day Fires on the Oregon Slender Salamander and its Habitat\n\nThe Oreg
 on Slender Salamander is a sensitive species endemic to Oregon\, and is hig
 hly associated with old growth forests in the west Cascades. On Labor Day w
 eekend in 2020\, the Beachie Creek\, Lionshead\, and Riverside fire collect
 ively burned a large area of forests on the western slopes of the Cascades.
  In my thesis\, I sought to understand how these fires altered the habitat 
 and affected populations of the Oregon Slender Salamander.\n\nMiranda Kubek
  ‘26 - ES-Biology\, Fey & Mellies Labs “Pondering the Orb: Community Assemb
 ly in Macroscopic Microbial Colonies”\n\nNostoc pruniforme is a cyanobacter
 ium which forms grapefruit-sized spherical colonies. Nostoc forms multiple 
 specialized cell types\, making it an interesting microbial parallel to mul
 ticellular organisms. The colonies also serve as a habitat for non-Nostoc m
 icrobes. In this talk\, I explore how cellular populations are distributed 
 throughout these unusually massive microbial systems.\n\n11:50 am- Snacks &
  Socializing\nnoon- Talk Begins
DTEND:20260417T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260417T190000Z
GEO:45.480779;-122.627995
LOCATION:Biology\, 19
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Biology SPARKLE Talks: Seniors Presenting About (their) Research\, 
 Knowledge\, Learning\, & Efforts
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_51818309875038
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/biology-sparkle-talks-2026
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Lecture
DESCRIPTION:Debating the nature of invisible forces has always been a popul
 ar pastime whether it has taken the form of theology or experimental physic
 s. In the classroom\, teachers often narrate the history of these debates w
 ith a good dose of positivism\, making modernity appear a tidy intellectual
  path that leads from the confessionalization processes of the Reformation\
 , through the secularization processes of the Enlightenment\, to the empiri
 cally rigorous (but drab and disenchanted) culture of our present age. Howe
 ver\, after years of research into the history of magical beliefs\, through
 out the English and Spanish-speaking world\, I find myself convinced of two
  things: First\, that early-modern intellectuals explored and debated magic
 al concepts with striking intellectual rigor\; and second\, that nineteenth
  and twentieth-century intellectuals (despite their supposed secularization
  and disenchantment) remained deeply invested in the study of magic. This t
 alk explores the supernatural obsessions of the past two centuries not as d
 eviations from modernity\, but as an essential part of modernity.\n\nSean M
 cEnroe is Professor of History at Southern Oregon University.  A historian 
 of the Atlantic world\, he specializes in religion\, ideology\, and state f
 ormation. His newest book\, A Troubled Marriage\, is a study of the literal
  and figurative marriages among European and indigenous communities through
 out North and South America\; From Colony to Nationhood in Mexico\, describ
 es the role of Indian leaders in the creation of modern towns and modern ci
 tizenship. He is currently at work on a study of new beliefs about science 
 and magic that mingled and spread in the Atlantic world during the nineteen
 th century.
DTEND:20260417T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260417T190000Z
GEO:45.480972;-122.630792
LOCATION:Eliot Hall\, 103
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Laboratories and Inquisitions: Science\, Magic\, and Religion in th
 e Modern Atlantic World
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52516807088381
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/laboratories-and-inquisitions-science-mag
 ic-and-religion-in-the-modern-atlantic-world
END:VEVENT
