BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Lecture
DESCRIPTION:In June 1967\, a miner named Casildo Condori wrote a birthday l
 etter to his wife Nancy from the rearguard of Che Guevara’s guerrilla colum
 n. When he died in a fire fight with the Bolivian army a few days later\, t
 he soldiers kept the letter and the money he sent home. Nancy\, fearing Cas
 ildo had abandoned their daughter\, eventually turned to the courts to trac
 k her husband down. Che’s diary barely mentions the young miner and a Cuban
  memorial uses the wrong name\, but Casildo was more than a footnote in the
  Cold War. In this talk\, I use an unexpected archive: the family courts of
  a small mining city\, to put Nancy\, Casildo\, and their peers in the cent
 er of their own revolutionary struggle to protect working families and the 
 keep resistance alive in the face of a violent state and a harsh physical l
 andscape. \n\nElena McGrath is Assistant Professor of History and Assistant
  Dean of Studies at Union College in Schenectady\, New York. Her research i
 nterests\, which started with a 2006 Reed History thesis advised by David G
 arrett\, include race\, gender\, and revolution in the Andes. She received 
 her PhD in Latin American History from the University of Wisconsin-Madison 
 in 2016 and her first book\, The Limits of Revolution: Worker Citizens in a
  Bolivian Mining City\, was published by the University of Texas Press in M
 arch.
DTEND:20260424T010000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260423T233000Z
GEO:45.480644;-122.627381
LOCATION:Psychology\, 105
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:The Last Letter of 'Red' Condori: Resistance in the Archives of Col
 d War Bolivia
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52516622671771
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/the-last-letter-of-red-condori-resistance
 -in-the-archives-of-cold-war-bolivia
END:VEVENT
