BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Performing Arts,Community
DESCRIPTION:Community Dance at Reed is a project that aims to bring togethe
 r members of the Reed College and broader Portland communities. We practice
  community dance as a mode of dancemaking and social intervention based on 
 the principles of collective creation. Weekly creative movement sessions—of
 fered one semester each academic year—are free and open to the public. No p
 revious dance experience necessary. These sessions offer a forum for dancin
 g and talking about social\, cultural\, and identity based issues. We welco
 me all bodies\, with a critical awareness of how race\, class\, ability\, g
 ender\, and sexuality impact the ways we relate to ourselves and one anothe
 r in public. Together\, we collaboratively create dance works and perform i
 n the dance department’s biannual concerts.\n\nFree and open to the public.
  No previous dance experience necessary. No registration required.
DTEND:20260424T023000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260424T011000Z
GEO:45.480983;-122.634105
LOCATION:Performing Arts Building\, 240 (Dance Studio)
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Community Dance at Reed
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52135856776510
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/copy-of-community-dance-at-reed
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Lecture
DESCRIPTION:Join the Biology department as seniors share their thesis resea
 rch and discuss their their experiences throughout their thesis journey.\n\
 nAndrew Nguyen ‘26 - Neuroscience\, Cerveny Lab\n\n“Munchy Microglia - Cell
 s that Eat Brainz”\n\nMicroglia are neuroimmune cells of the central nervou
 s system that are key players in neuroinflammation. Within the developing z
 ebrafish brain\, phagocytic microglia can be observed responding to neuron 
 death caused by developmental apoptosis and surgical insults. By comparing 
 how phagocytic microglia behave under normal or injured developmental conte
 xts\, my thesis investigates how these cells coordinate their behaviors thr
 ough space and time. \n \n\nJolie Jaffe ‘26 - Biology\, Fey Lab\n\n“The Eff
 ects of Noise Pollution on the Calling Patterns of The Northern Red Legged 
 Frog (Rana aurora)”\n\nI used playback experiments and Underwater Passive A
 coustic Monitoring techniques to understand how noise pollution impacts the
  Northern Red-Legged Frog (Rana aurora) at Oaks Bottom Wildlife Refuge.\n \
 n\nParis Harrell ‘26 - Biology\, Applewhite Lab\n\n“Exploring the Effects o
 f Maternal Recovered Acute Kidney Injury on Offspring Neurological Developm
 ent”\n\nIn a mouse model of rhabdomyolysis-induced acute kidney injury (RIA
 KI)\, breeding mice with a history of recovered RIAKI resulted in dams with
  impaired kidney function during pregnancy. Offspring were born at term but
  were 3 times more likely to die in the pre-weaning period and exhibited fa
 r 5x higher rates of spontaneous hydrocephalus in adulthood. This project’s
  goal is to identify any differences in the vascular and immunological prof
 ile of the adult offspring brain using immunostaining techniques in order t
 o identify potential developmental origins of disease in offspring of paren
 ts with recovered RIAKI. \n\n11:50 am- Snacks & Socializing\nnoon- Talk Beg
 ins
DTEND:20260424T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022028Z
DTSTART:20260424T190000Z
GEO:45.480779;-122.627995
LOCATION:Biology\, 19
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Biology SPARKLE Talks: Seniors Presenting About (their) Research\, 
 Knowledge\, Learning\, & Efforts
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52621623317226
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/biology-sparkle-talks-2-2026
END:VEVENT
