BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:Alumni
DESCRIPTION:Join the South Willamette Valley alumni chapter at Finley Natio
 nal Wildlife Refuge—walking\, birding\, and having a picnic in celebration 
 of spring!\n\nThe refuge is located approximately 10 miles south of Corvall
 is west of Highway 99W. It protects and manages over 50\,000 acres of nativ
 e Willamette Valley wet prairie and oak savannah. It supports thousands of 
 species and is known for fantastic birding and hiking. It preserves what th
 e Willamette Valley might have looked like when the Kalapuya\, indigenous N
 ative American peoples of the Willamette Valley\, used fire to manage the v
 alley's prairies for thousands of years. The refuge uses a variety of manag
 ement practices to preserve these rare habitats. \n\nThings to pack: a picn
 ic lunch\, weather appropriate clothing\, binoculars\, and sketching or art
  supplies. We will have a mindful scavenger hunt\, some watercolor supplies
  to share\, and dessert for everyone. This is a rain or shine event. There 
 is a covered pavilion with benches\; camp chairs are also a good idea. Kids
  and friends are welcome to join!\n\nDon’t forget to RSVP here. If you’re a
 ttending\, watch for an email from Ashley before the event with location/me
 eting point details. The address listed is not our meeting place. It is the
  address of the refuge offices. We will provide directions to meeting place
  in a group reminder to those who RSVP.\n\nHosted by Amanda ‘91\, Ashley ‘0
 7\, and Jewel ‘82
DTEND:20260426T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022029Z
DTSTART:20260426T190000Z
GEO:44.419814;-123.324397
LOCATION:Finley Wildlife Refuge
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Celebrate Spring! — Finley Wildlife Refuge + Picnic Lunch
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52559438139292
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/celebrate-spring
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
CATEGORIES:
DESCRIPTION:The story of global trade and shipping is often narrated as one
  of ever-increasing efficiency and speed\, of transitions from sail to stea
 m to cargo and futures of automation—of making straight lines. But\, for as
  long as there has been trade (and ships) there have been delays and interr
 uptions—rerouters and rerouting. I argue that disruptions and moments when 
 things appear to be stuck or rerouted are neither aberrations nor failures.
  Rerouting\, far from being an exception\, is both the norm and\, at times\
 , such as this contemporary moment of trade wars and resurgent forms of vio
 lence\, the condition desired by many as a mode of profit and possibility.\
 n\nTo showcase the productive possibilities of rerouting\, I focus on one s
 ea route—the Bab-el- Mandeb\, a key chokepoint for global trade connecting 
 Europe to Asia and the Red Sea to the Indian Ocean. In 2023\, in response t
 o Houthi attacks on merchant ships\, this shipping route briefly disappeare
 d\, as cargo ships were rerouted through the Cape of Good Hope. I tell this
  story of this rerouting and its consequences both ephemeral and long-lasti
 ng from the perspective of a mobile set of actors in the Red Sea. Locating 
 them within longer histories and wider geographies\, I argue for the centra
 lity of interruptions within our understandings of circulation and crossing
 s in the Indian Ocean and beyond.\n\nJatin Dua is an associate professor of
  Anthropology at the University of Michigan. He also directs the Oceans Lab
  at the University of Michigan dedicated to collaborative and multimodal wa
 ys of engaging oceans as anthropological interlocutors. His research explor
 es maritime mobility\, and its perils and possibilities\, in the Indian Oce
 an\, focusing on processes and projects of governance\, law\, and economy. 
 His book\, Captured at Sea: Piracy and Protection in the Indian Ocean\, pub
 lished with the University of California Press (December 2019) [winner of t
 he 2020 Elliot P. Skinner Book Award\; shortlisted for the 2024 Monsoon Boo
 k Prize and translated into Italian and Greek] is a multi-sited ethnographi
 c and archival engagement with Somali piracy and contestations over legitim
 ate and illegitimate commerce in the Western Indian Ocean. In addition\, he
  has published several articles on maritime anthropology\, captivity\, poli
 tical economy\, and sovereignty in Public Culture\, American Ethnologist\, 
 Cultural Anthropology\, Ethnos\, Annual Review of Anthropology\, History an
 d Anthropology and Comparative Studies in Society and History. He is curren
 tly the editor-in-chief of Comparative Studies in Society and History.
DTEND:20260428T003000Z
DTSTAMP:20260423T022029Z
DTSTART:20260427T233000Z
GEO:45.480644;-122.627381
LOCATION:Psychology\, 105
SEQUENCE:0
